          "secNumber": "2.4",
          "heading": "Topic 2.2 \u2014 Soil chemistry and the rhizosphere",
          "content": "### Mechanistic explanation\n\nThe plant-accessible pool of an ion depends on dissolution, adsorption, oxidation state, microbial activity and diffusion through moist soil. Phosphate can be immobilized by mineral surfaces; iron solubility responds strongly to pH and redox environment; ammonium and nitrate move and transform differently. Roots change their immediate environment through proton fluxes, organic exudates and associations with microbes.\n\n### Coupled constraints and functional trade-offs\n\nTotal elemental abundance in soil is not equivalent to supply at the root membrane. Drying lowers diffusion and alters microbial turnover even if a chemical assay still reports a sizable nutrient pool. Increasing fertilizer may fail when roots cannot access ions or when another constraint such as oxygen limitation suppresses uptake.\n\n### How evidence would be collected\n\nSample bulk soil and rhizosphere separately while recording water content and pH. Compare a fertilizer dose response in two contrasting substrates and check the dissolved fraction over time. To assess exudation, use controls for microbial use and recovery of compounds from the sampling medium.\n\n### Scope, assumptions and what must not be inferred\n\nExtractable soil nutrients depend on extraction protocols and are operational measures, not direct measurements of the exact flux entering roots. Do not generalize one soil-test threshold globally.\n\n### Diagnostic distinction\n\nIn **soil chemistry and the rhizosphere**, the central distinction is **rhizosphere availability**. Specify the treatment, one tissue-level mediator, and a direct measurement that could distinguish the proposed mechanism from an alternative involving **transport selectivity and ion homeostasis**. A time course and appropriate control are stronger evidence than a single endpoint.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 2.2\n\n**Question.** In a simple diffusion toy model J = D A \u0394C/L with D = 1e\u22129 m2/s, area 0.01 m2, concentration difference 2 mol/m3 and path 0.002 m. Compute J.\n\n**Relation.** `J = D A \u0394C/L`. Evaluate the supplied hypothetical quantities in consistent units. The calculated value is **1e-08 mol s\u22121**. Reconstruct the arithmetic independently and specify whether it estimates a flux, concentration, coefficient or fraction. A correct numerical answer without units and domain assumptions is incomplete.\n\n**Interpretation.** A thicker diffusion path reduces passive nutrient access. **Model boundary.** Soil diffusion is complex and this D is a made-up illustrative value. This is a synthetic calculation, not a measured field value. Replace one positive input with a value 20% greater, recompute, and ask whether an intact plant could instead respond through feedback that changes other inputs. When a model assumes constant conditions, a measurement during a rapid transient can deviate even if the mechanism is scientifically reasonable.\n\n### Test your explanation\n\nPropose a control for the measurement described above. Explain how plant age or environment could change the predicted response, and identify a result that would falsify your favored mechanism. Distinguish a directly measured quantity from one inferred through the equation.",
