          "secNumber": "2.6",
          "heading": "Topic 2.4 \u2014 Nitrogen fixation, assimilation and symbiosis",
          "content": "### Mechanistic explanation\n\nNitrogen supplied as nitrate or ammonium must be incorporated into organic molecules. Nitrate reduction, ammonium assimilation through glutamine synthetase and related reactions, and exchange between tissues link mineral supply to light-derived reducing power and carbon skeletons. Some plants host nitrogen-fixing bacteria; the enzyme nitrogenase requires considerable energy and protection from oxygen damage.\n\n### Coupled constraints and functional trade-offs\n\nA green, fast-growing legume may still obtain substantial nitrogen from soil rather than fixation. Nodules consume carbon supplied by the host, so symbiosis is a regulated exchange rather than a free nutrient source. A well-nodulated root under phosphorus shortage or heat stress may have low fixation despite apparently abundant nodules.\n\n### How evidence would be collected\n\nCompare nodulated and nonnodulated plants at defined soil nitrogen supplies; track dry mass, tissue nitrogen and a justified fixation assay. Use isotopic or mass-balance evidence when possible instead of interpreting nodule number as fixation rate. Distinguish photosynthetic carbon input from immediate respiratory energy cost.\n\n### Scope, assumptions and what must not be inferred\n\nAcetylene reduction can index nitrogenase activity under selected protocols but needs calibration and is not automatically a direct whole-season nitrogen-fixation value.\n\n### Diagnostic distinction\n\nIn **nitrogen fixation, assimilation and symbiosis**, the central distinction is **symbiotic nutrient exchange**. Specify the treatment, one tissue-level mediator, and a direct measurement that could distinguish the proposed mechanism from an alternative involving **diagnosis and nutrient-use efficiency**. A time course and appropriate control are stronger evidence than a single endpoint.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 2.4\n\n**Question.** A pot receives 14 mg nitrogen from solution and harvest tissue contains 8.4 mg of the added nitrogen. Estimate apparent recovery.\n\n**Relation.** `recovery = recovered / supplied \u00d7100`. Evaluate the supplied hypothetical quantities in consistent units. The calculated value is **60 %**. Reconstruct the arithmetic independently and specify whether it estimates a flux, concentration, coefficient or fraction. A correct numerical answer without units and domain assumptions is incomplete.\n\n**Interpretation.** A fraction of the supplied nutrient is traced into harvested biomass. **Model boundary.** Other tissue pools and losses may remain unaccounted. This is a synthetic calculation, not a measured field value. Replace one positive input with a value 20% greater, recompute, and ask whether an intact plant could instead respond through feedback that changes other inputs. When a model assumes constant conditions, a measurement during a rapid transient can deviate even if the mechanism is scientifically reasonable.\n\n### Test your explanation\n\nPropose a control for the measurement described above. Explain how plant age or environment could change the predicted response, and identify a result that would falsify your favored mechanism. Distinguish a directly measured quantity from one inferred through the equation.",
          "simulations": []
