          "secNumber": "4.4",
          "heading": "Topic 4.2 \u2014 Alternative oxidase and redox flexibility",
          "content": "### Mechanistic explanation\n\nPlant mitochondria can route electrons through alternative oxidase in addition to the cytochrome pathway. This alternative route can lower the proton-pumping yield associated with electron transfer while helping manage redox pressure in specific contexts. The role of alternative oxidase depends on regulatory state, substrate supply and stress exposure.\n\n### Coupled constraints and functional trade-offs\n\nHigher oxygen consumption need not indicate more ATP formation. If alternative flux rises, carbon may be oxidized with different coupling efficiency. Inhibitor-based partitioning can be distorted by changing substrate oxidation or compensatory responses, so evidence from expression alone also cannot establish functional flux.\n\n### How evidence would be collected\n\nCompare respiratory oxygen uptake with and without carefully controlled perturbations, supplemented by protein abundance and respiratory redox markers. Test growth and stress outcomes under independent conditions. Distinguish what occurs in excised tissues from the performance of intact plants over several days.\n\n### Scope, assumptions and what must not be inferred\n\nThis pathway is not a wasteful on/off bypass in all plants; simplified branching diagrams omit regulation and the distribution of electron sources.\n\n### Diagnostic distinction\n\nIn **alternative oxidase and redox flexibility**, the central distinction is **glycolysis and the TCA cycle**. Specify the treatment, one tissue-level mediator, and a direct measurement that could distinguish the proposed mechanism from an alternative involving **alternative respiratory pathways**. A time course and appropriate control are stronger evidence than a single endpoint.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 4.2\n\n**Question.** Mitochondrial oxygen uptake is 30 units of which 9 are carried through a hypothetical alternative branch. Estimate its fraction.\n\n**Relation.** `branch percentage = alternative/total \u00d7100`. Evaluate the supplied hypothetical quantities in consistent units. The calculated value is **30 %**. Reconstruct the arithmetic independently and specify whether it estimates a flux, concentration, coefficient or fraction. A correct numerical answer without units and domain assumptions is incomplete.\n\n**Interpretation.** Branching can decouple oxygen use from ATP yield. **Model boundary.** Inhibitor partition can alter flux and mislead. This is a synthetic calculation, not a measured field value. Replace one positive input with a value 20% greater, recompute, and ask whether an intact plant could instead respond through feedback that changes other inputs. When a model assumes constant conditions, a measurement during a rapid transient can deviate even if the mechanism is scientifically reasonable.\n\n### Test your explanation\n\nPropose a control for the measurement described above. Explain how plant age or environment could change the predicted response, and identify a result that would falsify your favored mechanism. Distinguish a directly measured quantity from one inferred through the equation.",
          "simulations": []
