          "secNumber": "5.4",
          "heading": "Topic 5.2 \u2014 Phloem pressure flow and sucrose loading",
          "content": "### Mechanistic explanation\n\nIn many species, sucrose is loaded into the sieve-tube\u2013companion-cell complex through apoplastic transporters or symplastic connections. Solute accumulation lowers water potential, draws water in and can build turgor that drives pressure-driven mass flow toward tissues where solutes are removed. Conductive sieve tubes are living tissue whose function depends on surrounding cells and local metabolic regulation.\n\n### Coupled constraints and functional trade-offs\n\nPressure flow moves solution, but unloading, loading and pressure generation are active and regulated. A tracer arriving in a sink is not equivalent to a direct measurement of bulk sap velocity if exchanges with intermediate pools occur. Sugar gradients alone do not establish the direction or speed of every individual sieve tube in a complex network.\n\n### How evidence would be collected\n\nUse noninvasive imaging or suitably controlled tracer movement together with sugar assays in source and sink tissues. Vary source supply while keeping sink demand comparable. Distinguish short-term tracer appearance from the slower consequences for growth and storage.\n\n### Scope, assumptions and what must not be inferred\n\nThe M\u00fcnch pressure-flow picture is an explanatory framework; it omits multicellular exchange, variable conduit geometry and dynamic sinks when used as a two-compartment toy model.\n\n### Diagnostic distinction\n\nIn **phloem pressure flow and sucrose loading**, the central distinction is **pressure-driven sieve-tube flow**. Specify the treatment, one tissue-level mediator, and a direct measurement that could distinguish the proposed mechanism from an alternative involving **loading and unloading pathways**. A time course and appropriate control are stronger evidence than a single endpoint.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 5.2\n\n**Question.** Phloem volume flow is 0.08 mL h\u22121 with sucrose concentration 0.55 mmol/mL. Compute sucrose movement.\n\n**Relation.** `solute flux = Q\u00d7c`. Evaluate the supplied hypothetical quantities in consistent units. The calculated value is **0.044 mmol h\u22121**. Reconstruct the arithmetic independently and specify whether it estimates a flux, concentration, coefficient or fraction. A correct numerical answer without units and domain assumptions is incomplete.\n\n**Interpretation.** Sugar concentration alone does not equal translocation. **Model boundary.** Flow and concentration must refer to the same route and interval. This is a synthetic calculation, not a measured field value. Replace one positive input with a value 20% greater, recompute, and ask whether an intact plant could instead respond through feedback that changes other inputs. When a model assumes constant conditions, a measurement during a rapid transient can deviate even if the mechanism is scientifically reasonable.\n\n### Test your explanation\n\nPropose a control for the measurement described above. Explain how plant age or environment could change the predicted response, and identify a result that would falsify your favored mechanism. Distinguish a directly measured quantity from one inferred through the equation.",
          "simulations": []
