          "secNumber": "6.2",
          "heading": "Measurement before mechanism",
          "content": "A reproducible plant-physiology experiment begins with specimen identity, developmental stage, time of measurement, environmental history and a declared biological replicate. For gas exchange or transpiration specify area basis and temperature. For solutes specify concentration versus total amount, fresh mass versus dry mass and intracellular compartment where known. For time series decide whether independent plants were destructively harvested or repeated measurements came from the same individual. Neither a spectacular photograph nor a perfectly smooth fitted curve establishes a causal mechanism by itself.\n\nThere are three useful levels of uncertainty. **Instrument uncertainty** concerns calibration and resolution; **sampling uncertainty** concerns variation among independently grown plants or plots; and **model uncertainty** concerns assumptions used to turn readings into rates or parameters. These should not be merged into one vague caveat. Synthetic datasets in this unit demonstrate analytical reasoning but cannot estimate real-world uncertainty. In appropriate classroom experiments, use benign and authorized materials, avoid harmful chemical handling and follow the institution's biological and greenhouse safety policies.",
