          "secNumber": "7.6",
          "heading": "Topic 7.4 \u2014 Dormancy, germination and reserve mobilization",
          "content": "### Mechanistic explanation\n\nViable seeds do not necessarily germinate immediately when provided with water. Dormancy includes physiological, morphological and physical constraints with species-specific release cues. Upon germination, imbibition restarts metabolism, respiration rises and hydrolytic enzymes mobilize reserves to sustain growth before the seedling is photosynthetically independent.\n\n### Coupled constraints and functional trade-offs\n\nFailure to germinate may indicate dormancy, nonviability or inappropriate assay conditions. A treatment that increases apparent germination could break dormancy or kill microbes that previously blocked emergence. Germination fraction and rate describe different dimensions of performance, particularly under temperature fluctuations.\n\n### How evidence would be collected\n\nUse defined seed lots, replicate trays, and an agreed criterion for radicle emergence. Record cumulative germination through time and assess remaining seeds for viability using an independent method when appropriate. Compare pretreatment, darkness, temperature and water availability systematically.\n\n### Scope, assumptions and what must not be inferred\n\nDo not infer dormancy class from a single failure. Some pretreatments are unsuitable or damaging for particular species and must be justified independently.\n\n### Diagnostic distinction\n\nIn **dormancy, germination and reserve mobilization**, the central distinction is **dormancy versus viability**. Specify the treatment, one tissue-level mediator, and a direct measurement that could distinguish the proposed mechanism from an alternative involving **senescence and nutrient remobilization**. A time course and appropriate control are stronger evidence than a single endpoint.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 7.4\n\n**Question.** By day 5, 62 of 80 seeds germinate. Estimate observed germination percentage.\n\n**Relation.** `germination = emerged/total\u00d7100`. Evaluate the supplied hypothetical quantities in consistent units. The calculated value is **77.5 %**. Reconstruct the arithmetic independently and specify whether it estimates a flux, concentration, coefficient or fraction. A correct numerical answer without units and domain assumptions is incomplete.\n\n**Interpretation.** Observed fraction is specific to conditions and time window. **Model boundary.** Ungerminated seeds may be dormant or dead. This is a synthetic calculation, not a measured field value. Replace one positive input with a value 20% greater, recompute, and ask whether an intact plant could instead respond through feedback that changes other inputs. When a model assumes constant conditions, a measurement during a rapid transient can deviate even if the mechanism is scientifically reasonable.\n\n### Test your explanation\n\nPropose a control for the measurement described above. Explain how plant age or environment could change the predicted response, and identify a result that would falsify your favored mechanism. Distinguish a directly measured quantity from one inferred through the equation.",
