          "secNumber": "7.7",
          "heading": "Topic 7.5 \u2014 Senescence, remobilization and abscission",
          "content": "### Mechanistic explanation\n\nLeaf senescence is a regulated developmental program involving breakdown of photosynthetic machinery, changes in hormones and movement of recoverable nutrients to active sinks. Abscission separates organs through specialized cell layers whose activation can be influenced by age, developmental signals and environmental stress. Senescence and stress injury may overlap but are not identical processes.\n\n### Coupled constraints and functional trade-offs\n\nYellowing is a visible symptom and not a complete measurement of nutrient remobilization. A leaf may decline in chlorophyll while still exporting amino acids, and premature abscission can reduce canopy carbon gain. Conversely, delayed senescence can help photosynthesis but may hinder maturation or remobilization under some circumstances.\n\n### How evidence would be collected\n\nTag cohorts of leaves at known ages and follow chlorophyll, nitrogen content and photosynthetic rate through time. Collect shed tissue and remaining plant organs to calculate a partial nutrient budget. Compare induced shade or drought with age-matched controls to separate developmental senescence from injury.\n\n### Scope, assumptions and what must not be inferred\n\nStay-green color does not guarantee sustained carbon assimilation. Total nutrient recovery needs whole-plant inventory, not just a falling concentration in the original leaf.\n\n### Diagnostic distinction\n\nIn **senescence, remobilization and abscission**, the central distinction is **senescence and nutrient remobilization**. Specify the treatment, one tissue-level mediator, and a direct measurement that could distinguish the proposed mechanism from an alternative involving **flowering cues and photoperiod**. A time course and appropriate control are stronger evidence than a single endpoint.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 7.5\n\n**Question.** A leaf has 12 mg nitrogen before senescence, 4.5 mg at abscission; estimate apparent removal.\n\n**Relation.** `apparent remobilization=(initial\u2212final)/initial\u00d7100`. Evaluate the supplied hypothetical quantities in consistent units. The calculated value is **62.5 %**. Reconstruct the arithmetic independently and specify whether it estimates a flux, concentration, coefficient or fraction. A correct numerical answer without units and domain assumptions is incomplete.\n\n**Interpretation.** A decline in old-leaf nutrient amount suggests export or losses. **Model boundary.** Leaching and sampling differences require controls. This is a synthetic calculation, not a measured field value. Replace one positive input with a value 20% greater, recompute, and ask whether an intact plant could instead respond through feedback that changes other inputs. When a model assumes constant conditions, a measurement during a rapid transient can deviate even if the mechanism is scientifically reasonable.\n\n### Test your explanation\n\nPropose a control for the measurement described above. Explain how plant age or environment could change the predicted response, and identify a result that would falsify your favored mechanism. Distinguish a directly measured quantity from one inferred through the equation.",
